# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chemlab environment configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and adjust as needed:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Every variable below is OPTIONAL — the app runs with no env file at all and
# falls back to the defaults shown here. `.env.local` is git-ignored.
#
# All variables are prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, which means Next.js inlines them
# into the browser bundle at BUILD time. They are visible to anyone who loads
# the site, so never put a secret behind this prefix, and rebuild after
# changing one — restarting the server is not enough.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Canonical origin of the deployment. Used for metadataBase, the canonical
# link, Open Graph / Twitter URLs, and the absolute OG image URL.
# Set this to your real domain in production, otherwise social cards and
# canonical URLs point at localhost.
# Must be an absolute URL including the scheme.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Product name shown in page titles, metadata and the UI.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_NAME=Chemlab

# Description used for the meta description and Open Graph / Twitter cards.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_DESCRIPTION="Chemlab is a fun, interactive, and kid-friendly web app to explore chemistry. Learn atoms, molecules, elements, reactions, and take random quizzes—all without an account."

# Optional Twitter/X handle for the twitter:site card tag, including the "@".
# Leave unset to omit the tag entirely.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_HANDLE=@ChemlabApp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (server-only — NEVER prefix these NEXT_PUBLIC_)
#
# A NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable is inlined into the JavaScript every visitor
# downloads, and a Neon connection string carries its password inline. Setting
# either of these as NEXT_PUBLIC_ publishes your database credentials.
#
# The app runs fine with both unset: `pnpm dev`, `pnpm build` and the test
# suite need no database. Set them when you want to run migrations or query
# real data.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# LOCAL Postgres (the default for development)
#
# The repo ships with a local cluster; no cloud account is needed:
#
#   pnpm db:local:start   # start PostgreSQL on 127.0.0.1:5432
#   pnpm env:check        # confirm what the app will actually connect to
#   pnpm db:migrate       # apply db/migrations
#   pnpm db:seed          # load the JSON content (safe to re-run)
#   pnpm db:check         # prove connectivity and report applied migrations
#   pnpm db:local:reset   # drop everything and start over (local hosts only)
#
# The driver is chosen from the host: a *.neon.tech host uses the Neon
# serverless driver, anything else uses node-postgres over TCP. Locally both
# variables can point at the same URL — there is no connection pooler in
# front of a local cluster, so there is no direct/pooled distinction.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://chemlab:chemlab@127.0.0.1:5432/chemlab
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=postgresql://chemlab:chemlab@127.0.0.1:5432/chemlab

# NOTE ON PRECEDENCE: unlike stock Next.js, `.env.local` OVERRIDES a variable
# that is already set in the shell (see lib/load-env.ts). Hosted dev
# containers sometimes inject a DATABASE_URL of their own, and without this
# the file you just edited would silently do nothing. `.env.local` is
# git-ignored and never deployed, so this cannot affect production.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hosted Postgres (Neon) — optional alternative to the local cluster
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Neon POOLED endpoint — note the "-pooler" in the host. Used by the app.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/chemlab?sslmode=require

# Neon DIRECT endpoint — no "-pooler". Used only by drizzle-kit, migrations
# and seed scripts: PgBouncer in transaction mode cannot hold the session-level
# locks that DDL needs.
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx.region.aws.neon.tech/chemlab?sslmode=require

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication (server-only — NEVER prefix these NEXT_PUBLIC_)
#
# All four are OPTIONAL. Without them the site serves every public page and the
# account UI simply does not appear — `pnpm env:check` reports which of
# "email/password and Google", "email/password only" or "disabled" you have.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs session cookies and tokens. Generate once and keep it:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Rotating it invalidates every existing session.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# The canonical origin auth runs on. It must match the origin in the Google
# redirect URI EXACTLY, or the callback fails with an error that reads like a
# configuration problem and is one.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google OAuth — both halves or neither
#
# The owner creates these in the Google Cloud console; see docs/AUTH.md for the
# exact consent-screen and redirect-URI settings. The redirect URI must be
# registered verbatim as:
#
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
#   https://<production-domain>/api/auth/callback/google
#
# Google accepts no wildcards, so every origin that needs Google sign-in must
# be listed. Put the real values in .env.local or the deployment's environment
# — never in this file, an issue, or a pull request.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The first Super Admin (server-only)
#
# Granting a role requires being a Super Admin, so the first grant cannot
# happen through the app. Sign up normally with this address, then run:
#
#   pnpm db:bootstrap-admin
#
# The script never creates the account — that way the password is hashed by
# Better Auth and never exists in a script, an env var, or a shell history.
# See docs/PERMISSIONS.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL=owner@example.com
